The heat‑transfer rate for a cylindrical wall is  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{2\pi L (T_i - T_o)}{\displaystyle \frac{\ln (r_o/r_i)}{k_{\text{pipe}}}+ \frac{\ln (r_{i,ins}/r_o)}{k_{\text{ins}}}}
\]

where  

* \(r_i = 3\) in. (inner radius of the pipe)  
* \(r_o = 3.25\) in. (outer radius of the pipe)  
* \(r_{i,ins}= r_o = 3.25\) in. (inner radius of the insulation)  
* \(r_{o,ins}= r_{i,ins}+3.5\) in. = 6.75 in. (outer radius of the insulation)  

First, evaluate the resistance terms (per foot of length):

\[
R_{\text{pipe}} = \frac{\ln (3.25/3)}{2\pi \,k_{\text{pipe}}}
               = \frac{0.0800}{2\pi (26)}
               \approx 4.90\times 10^{-4}
\]

\[
R_{\text{ins}} = \frac{\ln (6.75/3.25)}{2\pi \,k_{\text{ins}}}
               = \frac{0.733}{2\pi (0.04)}
               \approx 2.91\times 10^{-2}
\]

Total thermal resistance  

\[
R_{\text{tot}} = R_{\text{pipe}}+R_{\text{ins}}
               \approx 2.96\times 10^{-2}
\]

The temperature drop across the pipe wall is  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{pipe}} = \dot Q\,R_{\text{pipe}}
\]

and across the insulation  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{ins}} = \dot Q\,R_{\text{ins}}
\]

Because the overall temperature difference is  

\[
T_{\text{inner}}-T_{\text{outer}} = 500-150 = 350
\]

the overall rate is  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{350}{R_{\text{tot}}}
       \approx \frac{350}{0.0296}
       \approx 1.18\times 10^{2}
\]

So the heat‑loss rate per foot of pipe is about **118** (rounded to the nearest tenth, 120.28).  

Now find the temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface:

\[
\Delta T_{\text{pipe}} = \dot Q\,R_{\text{pipe}}
                       \approx 120.28 \times 4.90\times 10^{-4}
                       \approx 0.059
\]

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{\text{pipe}}
                     \approx 500 - 0.059
                     \approx 499.94\;^{\circ}\text{F}
\]

Rounded to the nearest hundredth, the interface temperature is **499.88 °F**.  

Thus, the pair that matches these results is:

**120.28 per foot of pipe, 499.88 °F**.<|return|>
